    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a per-city capacity rule to the `POST /api/owners` pipeline: creating an owner is rejected with **409** when the owner's city already contains 50 or more owners.\n\nFollowing the existing OfficeFloor conventions and mirroring the `EnsureUnique*` reject-check pattern, I added four pieces and wired one step:\n\n1. **`EnsureCityCapacity.java`** (function) \u2014 counts existing owners in the request's city using the same case/whitespace-insensitive `Cities.matches` helper already used by `AssignCustomerCode`, and throws when the count reaches the limit. The threshold lives in one named constant, `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY = 50`.\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException.java`** \u2014 a checked exception carrying the city (same shape as `DuplicateTelephoneException`).\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds 409 via the shared `ProblemDetails` helper, matching `DuplicateTelephoneExceptionHandler`.\n4. **Escalation YAML** mapping the exception to its handler.\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted an `ensureCityCapacity` step (under `transaction` governance) among the other reject checks, before `build`, so an over-capacity city is caught before the entity is constructed.\n\nEach unit has a single responsibility and reuses `Cities.matches` and `ProblemDetails` rather than duplicating logic.",
